<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibn Arafa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1316–1401</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibn Arafa was a jurist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, the leading religious scholar of the Hafsid capital in the fourteenth century. He served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-zaytuna-mosque">Zaytuna Mosque</a> and mufti of Tunis, and his works on Maliki law, especially his concise legal compendium, were studied across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> for centuries. He was a teacher and later a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-arafa.jpg" alt="Ibn Arafa" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rais67 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tunis_Djellaz_Ibn_Arfa.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
